  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeep upper level trough will maintain its influence over Southeast \nMichigan throughout the day. Combination of warm lake water \ntemperatures with tight cyclonic flow will continue to bring lake \neffect or bay effect rain chances to the Thumb this morning. The \noutstanding question is when rain will eventually pull away from \nHuron County today. Forecast soundings support a fair amount of \nwarming in the 3.0 to 6.0 kft agl layer after 15z as lower heights \npush eastward. The net result is that higher midlevel stability \nworking into the top of the mixed layer should cause lake/bay effect \nto become more sparse by the afternoon today. Highs today are \nexpected to be in the middle to upper 70s which is 2 to 4 degrees \nbelow normal.\n\nSurface high pressure will build across the region tonight and \nTuesday. Ideal lat e summer for Tuesday with daytime highs near 80 \ndegrees and dewpoints in the 50s.\n\nWarm front will lift across Lower Michigan Tuesday night and early \nWednesday as broad troughing settles into the upper Midwest. Models \nhave been very consistent in showing nothing more than a very weak \nupper level jet streak. Big picture perspective suggests that \nprecipitation chances will rely heavily on when direct differential \ncyclonic vorticity arrives. This suggests sometime late in the \nWednesday period particularly for areas south of I 69. This looks \ngood as residual stability and dry air from strong subsidence the \nday before will be place Wednesday afternoon. The current forecast \ncalls for dry or a slight chance in Detroit prior to 00z Thursday. \nModels then suggest a good amount of wave dampening which results in \nnothing more than a spoke of absolute vorticity early Thursday, \nunorganized embedded wave energy late Thursday and early Friday. \nGood confidence slightly more humid weather Thursday with dewpoints \nthen dropping behind a cold front Friday.\n\nLower confidence exists regarding the forecast next weekend. There \nis some global deterministic differences on when the next Pacific \njet packet will push into the Great Lakes region. GFS suggests as \nearly as Saturday while the ECMWF waits until Sunday. Temperatures \nlook to be in the 80s next weekend.",
